  "answer": "<p>The <strong>correct answer</strong> is: <b>Create a single AWS CloudFormation template to manage your infrastructure in version control, and use this template to deploy the Docker container into Elastic Beanstalk in both staging and production environments.</b></p><p>This approach is recommended because:</p><ul><li>It uses AWS CloudFormation to codify your infrastructure, which provides a version-controlled, repeatable, and reliable method for provisioning AWS resources.</li><li>By deploying to Elastic Beanstalk, it abstracts much of the infrastructure management required for running Docker containers, further aligning your deployment architecture across all environments.</li><li>Utilizing a single template for both staging and production ensures a consistent configuration, minimizing errors caused by environment drift.</li></ul><p>Why other options are incorrect or less optimal:</p><ul><li><i>Employ the AWS SDK or CLI with a custom script to push code from version control to various environments, and utilize AWS OpsWorks for deployments</i>: While possible, this approach lacks the desired infrastructure-as-code (IaC) benefits that CloudFormation and Elastic Beanstalk provide, such as repeatability, rollback, and easy environment creation. Using OpsWorks can also be unnecessarily complex compared to Elastic Beanstalk for Docker deployments.</li><li><i>Since the app runs inside a Docker container, assume infrastructure parity between local setups and AWS, requiring no additional deployment steps for staging or production</i>: This is incorrect, as differences can still exist in networking, storage, security settings, and scaling between local and AWS environments. A robust, managed deployment process is still necessary for production-grade reliability.</li><li><i>Construct an AWS CloudFormation template for each lifecycle environment—development, testing, and production—and use resource tagging in each template to specify the environment type</i>: While this offers some environment control, managing multiple templates can lead to duplication, increased maintenance, and potential inconsistencies. A single version-controlled template with environment parameters is a best practice.</li></ul><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Versioning your CloudFormation templates and leveraging Elastic Beanstalk's Docker support allows for robust, automated, and consistent deployments—critical for professional DevOps workflows.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You have established a web application development environment via a third-party configuration management solution, which creates a Docker container on each developer's local machine. What is the <strong>best</strong> approach to ensure consistent and reliable deployment of your application across local, staging, and production environments on AWS?</p>",
